IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `hprnlq-dates`
IMPORT `hprnlq-complainant`

-- Health Practitioner Regulation National Law Act 2009 (Qld) and the National Law (Queensland) -- worked cases, from
-- the seat of the complainant.
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Expected values were written by hand
-- from the deposited text (act-2009-045 for the Act's own provisions and Part 4; act-2009-hprnlq for the Law as
-- modified), never copied from the encoding's output. No scenario life raised this Act; the facts are the encoder's,
-- HYPOTHETICAL throughout. Every judgment of a Board, a panel, the health ombudsman or a court is given as a fact.
--
-- Weekdays were worked by hand from 1 January 2026 (a Thursday) and 1 January 2028 (a Saturday): 4 May 2026 is a Monday,
-- 2 May 2026 a Saturday, 11 July 2026 a Saturday, 28 February 2026 a Saturday, 28 February 2027 a Sunday.
--
-- A breached DEONTIC cannot be compared with EQUALS on the stable l4, so the two regulative rules are asserted through
-- CONSIDER ... WHEN FULFILLED.

-- HYPOTHETICAL: Mere had shoulder surgery at a Brisbane private hospital. Her surgeon, Dr Kaur, has specialist
-- registration and practises mainly in Brisbane. Mere thinks the operation was done badly, and a theatre nurse told her
-- Dr Kaur had been drinking.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 7 of the Act, s 146, s 9 of the Act: which Queensland Acts reach the National Law (Queensland).
#ASSERT `Does this Queensland Act apply to the National Law question` `the Information Privacy Act 2009` EQUALS `answered` `no: section 7 of the Act says this Act does not apply to the National Law (Queensland) or to the instruments made under it`
#ASSERT `Does this Queensland Act apply to the National Law question` `the Right to Information Act 2009` EQUALS `answered` `no: section 7 of the Act says this Act does not apply to the National Law (Queensland) or to the instruments made under it`
#ASSERT `Does this Queensland Act apply to the National Law question` `the Ombudsman Act 2001` EQUALS `answered` `no: section 7 of the Act says this Act does not apply to the National Law (Queensland) or to the instruments made under it`
#ASSERT `Does this Queensland Act apply to the National Law question` `the Acts Interpretation Act 1954` EQUALS `answered` `no: section 7 of the Act says this Act does not apply to the National Law (Queensland) or to the instruments made under it`
#ASSERT `Does this Queensland Act apply to the National Law question` `the Statutory Instruments Act 1992` EQUALS `answered` `no: section 7 of the Act says this Act does not apply to the National Law (Queensland) or to the instruments made under it`
#ASSERT `Does this Queensland Act apply to the National Law question` `the Health Ombudsman Act 2013` EQUALS `answered` `yes: the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 applies to a notification (complaint) under Part 8 Divisions 2 and 3, and the health ombudsman deals with it as a complaint under part 3, division 2 of that Act (National Law (Queensland) s 146)`
#ASSERT `Does this Queensland Act apply to the National Law question` `the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2009 (the QCAT Act)` EQUALS `answered` `yes, for appeals: an appeal to QCAT under the National Law (Queensland) is a review of the decision under the QCAT Act (s 9 of the Act; QCAT is the responsible tribunal, s 6)`
-- The list in s 7 is not closed with "only": an unlisted Act is not covered.
#ASSERT `Does this Queensland Act apply to the National Law question` `another Queensland Act` EQUALS `not covered` `section 7 of the Act does not list this Act; whether it applies to the National Law (Queensland) turns on that Act and the general law, not encoded`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ss 138-139B: can a complaint be made about this person. Arguments: registration, when it happened, prior Act allowed.
`registered now` MEANS `registered now under this Law (general, specialist, provisional, limited or non-practising registration)`
`while registered` MEANS `while the person was registered`
`in a gap` MEANS `during a gap in registration (for example after it ended, lapsed, or was suspended or cancelled)`
`prior Act only` MEANS `registered only under a corresponding prior Act (before this Law), never under this Law`

#ASSERT `Can a complaint be made about this person question` `registered now` `while registered` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: a notification may be made, and a matter the health ombudsman refers may be dealt with, about a registered health practitioner for behaviour while registered (s 138(1)(a), (1A))`
#ASSERT `Can a complaint be made about this person question` `registered now` `before the person was first registered` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: a notification may be made, and a referred matter dealt with, for behaviour before registration (s 138(1)(b), (1A)), but that behaviour cannot be unsatisfactory professional performance or unprofessional conduct (s 138(2))`
#ASSERT `Can a complaint be made about this person question` `registered now` `in a gap` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: a notification may be made, and a referred matter dealt with, for behaviour in a gap in registration (s 138(1)(c), (1A)); it cannot be unsatisfactory professional performance, nor unprofessional conduct except where s 139 applies (practising on after registration ended under s 108(2)(a)) (s 138(2))`
#ASSERT `Can a complaint be made about this person question` `registered now as a student` `while registered` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: a notification may be made about a registered student, on the grounds in s 144(2)`
-- Dr Kaur retires; the operation was while registered.
#ASSERT `Can a complaint be made about this person question` `registered under this Law before, but not now` `while registered` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: a notification may be made, and a referred matter dealt with, about a former practitioner for behaviour while registered, as if still registered (s 139A(2), (2A)); Part 8 applies except Divisions 2 and 6 (s 139A(3))`
#ASSERT `Can a complaint be made about this person question` `registered under this Law before, but not now` `in a gap` FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `section 139A reaches a former practitioner's behaviour only while the person was registered; this Law does not say what may be done about behaviour after registration ended`
#ASSERT `Can a complaint be made about this person question` `prior Act only` `while registered` TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes: a notification may be made, and a referred matter dealt with, about a person registered under a corresponding prior Act for behaviour while so registered, to the extent one could have been made under that Act (s 139B(2), (2A), (3)); Part 8 applies except Divisions 2 and 7 (s 139B(4))`
#ASSERT `Can a complaint be made about this person question` `prior Act only` `while registered` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: for a person registered only under a corresponding prior Act, s 139B(2) and (2A) apply only to the extent a notification could have been made, and proceedings taken, under that Act (s 139B(3))`
#ASSERT `Can a complaint be made about this person question` `prior Act only` `in a gap` TRUE EQUALS `not covered` `section 139A reaches a former practitioner's behaviour only while the person was registered; this Law does not say what may be done about behaviour after registration ended`
#ASSERT `Can a complaint be made about this person question` `never registered as a health practitioner` `while registered` TRUE EQUALS `not covered` `Part 8 speaks of a person registered under this Law, or formerly registered, or registered under a corresponding prior Act; a person never registered is dealt with under the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 and Part 8 Division 7A (interim prohibition orders), not encoded`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ss 143A-146: can I notify the health ombudsman. Arguments: about, in Queensland, based in Queensland, (a) conduct,
-- (b) care, (c) unsuitable, (d) impairment, (e) contravened the Law, (f) condition, (g) improperly registered, student
-- charged or convicted, maximum months.
`practitioner` MEANS `a registered health practitioner`
`student` MEANS `a registered student`
`yes, practitioner` MEANS `answered` `yes: you may notify the health ombudsman, because you believe a ground in s 144(1) exists (ss 144(1), 145); the health ombudsman must deal with it under the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 as a complaint under part 3, division 2 of that Act (s 146(2))`
`yes, student` MEANS `answered` `yes: you may notify the health ombudsman about the student, because you believe a ground in s 144(2) exists (ss 144(2), 145); the health ombudsman must deal with it under the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 as a complaint under part 3, division 2 of that Act (s 146(2))`
`no ground` MEANS `not covered` `section 144 does not list the matter you describe as a ground; you may still be able to make a health service complaint under the Health Ombudsman Act 2013, not encoded`

-- Mere: the operation was in Brisbane, Dr Kaur is based there; care below standard.
#ASSERT `Can I make a voluntary notification question` `practitioner` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `yes, practitioner`
-- Each s 144(1) ground alone.
#ASSERT `Can I make a voluntary notification question` `practitioner` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `yes, practitioner`
#ASSERT `Can I make a voluntary notification question` `practitioner` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `yes, practitioner`
#ASSERT `Can I make a voluntary notification question` `practitioner` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `yes, practitioner`
#ASSERT `Can I make a voluntary notification question` `practitioner` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `yes, practitioner`
#ASSERT `Can I make a voluntary notification question` `practitioner` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `yes, practitioner`
#ASSERT `Can I make a voluntary notification question` `practitioner` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE 0 EQUALS `yes, practitioner`
-- Either Queensland link is enough (s 143A(1) "or"): conduct interstate by a Brisbane-based practitioner; conduct in
-- Queensland by a practitioner based in New South Wales.
#ASSERT `Can I make a voluntary notification question` `practitioner` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `yes, practitioner`
#ASSERT `Can I make a voluntary notification question` `practitioner` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `yes, practitioner`
-- Neither link: Division 3 does not apply in Queensland, even with a ground.
#ASSERT `Can I make a voluntary notification question` `practitioner` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `answered` `no: in Queensland, Division 3 applies to a practitioner only if the notification concerns conduct in Queensland or the practitioner's principal place of practice is in Queensland (s 143A(1)); notify under the National Law as it applies where the conduct happened`
#ASSERT `Can I make a voluntary notification question` `student` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `answered` `no: in Queensland, Division 3 applies to a student only if the notification concerns conduct in Queensland or the student studies or trains in Queensland (s 143A(2)); notify under the National Law as it applies where the conduct happened`
-- No ground: a billing dispute only.
#ASSERT `Can I make a voluntary notification question` `practitioner` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `no ground`
-- Student: an offence punishable by exactly 12 months is a ground; by 11 months is not.
#ASSERT `Can I make a voluntary notification question` `student` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE 12 EQUALS `yes, student`
#ASSERT `Can I make a voluntary notification question` `student` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE 11 EQUALS `no ground`
-- Student charged with nothing, but the maximum is high: no ground (s 144(2)(a) needs a charge or a conviction).
#ASSERT `Can I make a voluntary notification question` `student` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 24 EQUALS `no ground`
#ASSERT `Can I make a voluntary notification question` `student` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `yes, student`
#ASSERT `Can I make a voluntary notification question` `student` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `yes, student`
-- A practitioner-only ground (care) does not make a student notification (s 144(2) has no such ground).
#ASSERT `Can I make a voluntary notification question` `student` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `no ground`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ss 139D-142: was someone required to notify. Arguments: who knew, in Queensland, what they believed, risk of harm,
-- substantial, reasonable belief, in the course of practising, an exception applies.
`colleague` MEANS `a registered health practitioner who was not treating the practitioner (for example a colleague)`
`treating` MEANS `a registered health practitioner who was providing a health service to the practitioner (their treating practitioner)`
`employer` MEANS `the practitioner's employer (including an entity engaging them under a contract for services)`
`intoxicated` MEANS `the practitioner practised while intoxicated by alcohol or drugs`
`sexual misconduct` MEANS `the practitioner engaged, or is engaging, in sexual misconduct in connection with their practice`
`at risk of sexual misconduct` MEANS `the practitioner is only at risk of engaging in sexual misconduct in connection with their practice`
`impaired` MEANS `the practitioner practised while they had an impairment`
`departure` MEANS `the practitioner practised in a way that was a significant departure from accepted professional standards`
`none` MEANS `none of these (for example a single poor outcome, rudeness, or a billing dispute)`
`s 141 yes` MEANS `answered` `yes: the practitioner must notify the health ombudsman as soon as practicable after forming the reasonable belief (s 141(2)); a failure is not an offence but may be behaviour for which action may be taken under Part 8 or the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 (s 141(3))`
`s 141A yes` MEANS `answered` `yes: the treating practitioner must notify the health ombudsman of the sexual misconduct (engaged in, engaging in, or at risk of) as soon as practicable (s 141A(2)); a failure is not an offence but may lead to action under Part 8 or the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 (s 141A(3))`
`s 141B yes` MEANS `answered` `yes: the treating practitioner must notify the health ombudsman as soon as practicable, because the practitioner is placing the public at substantial risk of harm (s 141B(1), (2)); a failure is not an offence but may lead to action under Part 8 or the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 (s 141B(6))`
`s 142 yes` MEANS `answered` `yes: the employer must notify the health ombudsman of the notifiable conduct (s 142(1)); if it does not, the health ombudsman must tell the National Agency and may refer the employer to its licensing authority or another entity, or advise the Minister (s 142(2))`
`not notifiable` MEANS `answered` `no: what was believed is not notifiable conduct as s 140 defines it`
`exception` MEANS `answered` `no: the belief is taken not to have been formed in the course of practising (s 141(4)) or of providing a health service (s 141C(2)), for example because it was formed giving legal advice, for an indemnity insurer, on a quality assurance committee, or knowing the health ombudsman had already been told`
`treating no` MEANS `answered` `no: a treating practitioner is not required to notify under s 141 (s 141(2A)); under ss 141A and 141B a treating practitioner must notify only sexual misconduct (s 141A) or a substantial risk of harm to the public (s 141B)`

-- The theatre nurse (a registered practitioner, not treating Dr Kaur) reasonably believed Dr Kaur operated while drunk.
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `colleague` TRUE `intoxicated` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 141 yes`
-- The hospital (Dr Kaur's employer under a contract for services) reasonably believed the same.
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `employer` TRUE `intoxicated` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 142 yes`
-- s 139D(1): conduct outside Queensland.
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `colleague` FALSE `intoxicated` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: in Queensland, Division 2 applies to a practitioner's notifiable conduct only if the conduct occurs in Queensland (s 139D(1))`
-- A suspicion only.
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `employer` TRUE `intoxicated` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: the duty arises only when a reasonable belief is formed (ss 141(1), 141A(1), 141B(1), 142(1))`
-- A patient or relative: Division 2 does not put the duty on them.
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `someone else (for example a patient, a relative, or a manager who is not the employer)` TRUE `intoxicated` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `this Law does not say; Division 2 of Part 8 puts the duty to notify only on registered health practitioners, employers and education providers`
-- s 141(1): belief formed outside practice (at a party).
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `colleague` TRUE `intoxicated` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: s 141 applies only to a belief formed in the course of practising the profession (s 141(1))`
-- s 141(4)(e): the colleague knows the health ombudsman has been told.
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `colleague` TRUE `intoxicated` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `exception`
-- The s 141(4) exceptions do not reach the employer.
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `employer` TRUE `intoxicated` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `s 142 yes`
-- s 140(d): a significant departure needs a risk of harm, but not a substantial one.
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `colleague` TRUE `departure` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 141 yes`
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `colleague` TRUE `departure` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not notifiable`
-- s 140(c): an impairment needs a substantial risk.
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `colleague` TRUE `impaired` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 141 yes`
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `colleague` TRUE `impaired` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not notifiable`
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `employer` TRUE `impaired` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not notifiable`
-- s 140(b): sexual misconduct; but being only "at risk" of it is not notifiable conduct.
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `employer` TRUE `sexual misconduct` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 142 yes`
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `colleague` TRUE `at risk of sexual misconduct` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not notifiable`
-- A single poor outcome.
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `colleague` TRUE `none` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not notifiable`
-- Treating practitioners: s 141A covers being at risk of sexual misconduct; s 141B needs a substantial risk.
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `treating` TRUE `sexual misconduct` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 141A yes`
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `treating` TRUE `at risk of sexual misconduct` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 141A yes`
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `treating` TRUE `intoxicated` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 141B yes`
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `treating` TRUE `impaired` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 141B yes`
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `treating` TRUE `departure` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 141B yes`
-- Intoxicated practice is notifiable conduct (s 140(a)) without any risk; a treating practitioner still need not
-- notify it unless the risk is substantial (s 141(2A), s 141B(1)(b)).
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `treating` TRUE `intoxicated` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `treating no`
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `treating` TRUE `departure` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `treating no`
-- s 141C(2): a treating practitioner advising for a legal proceeding.
#ASSERT `Was someone required to notify question` `treating` TRUE `sexual misconduct` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `exception`

