-- ss 159A-192: what I must be told.
#ASSERT `What must I be told about the decision question` `the Board decided to investigate (s 160)` EQUALS `the Board must give you written notice of the progress of the investigation at not less than 3 monthly intervals (s 161(3)(b)); the exceptions in s 161(4) cover only notices to the practitioner or student`
#ASSERT `What must I be told about the decision question` `the Board took immediate action (for example suspended the practitioner or imposed conditions) (s 156)` EQUALS `the Board may tell you of the decision and its reasons, but need not (s 159A(2))`
#ASSERT `What must I be told about the decision question` `the Board decided what to do after an investigation (s 167)` EQUALS `the Board may tell you of the decision and its reasons, but need not (s 167A(2))`
#ASSERT `What must I be told about the decision question` `the Board decided what to do after a health or performance assessment report (s 177)` EQUALS `the Board may tell you of the decision and its reasons, but need not (s 177A(2))`
#ASSERT `What must I be told about the decision question` `the Board took action after the show cause process (caution, undertaking, conditions, reprimand and the like) (s 179(2))` EQUALS `the Board must give you written notice of the decision as soon as practicable; it may also give the reasons (s 180(1)(b), (2))`
#ASSERT `What must I be told about the decision question` `a health panel or performance and professional standards panel decided the matter (s 191 or s 191A)` EQUALS `the Board must give you written notice of the panel's decision within 30 days after the panel makes it; it may also give the reasons (s 192(2)(b), (4))`

-- s 161(3)(b): at least every 3 months. From Wednesday 15 January 2026: Wednesday 15 April 2026.
#ASSERT `By when must I next hear of the investigation question` (`the day` 15 1 2026) EQUALS `the day` 15 4 2026
-- From 30 November 2025: no 30 February, so 28 February 2026, a Saturday; moved to Monday 2 March 2026.
#ASSERT `By when must I next hear of the investigation question` (`the day` 30 11 2025) EQUALS `the day` 2 3 2026
-- From 30 November 2026: 28 February 2027, a Sunday; moved to Monday 1 March 2027.
#ASSERT `By when must I next hear of the investigation question` (`the day` 30 11 2026) EQUALS `the day` 1 3 2027

-- s 192(2)(b): 30 days. Panel decided Wednesday 10 June 2026: 20 days to 30 June, 10 in July: Friday 10 July 2026.
#ASSERT `By when must I be told of the panel decision question` (`the day` 10 6 2026) EQUALS `the day` 10 7 2026
-- Decided Thursday 11 June 2026: day 30 is Saturday 11 July; moved to Monday 13 July 2026.
#ASSERT `By when must I be told of the panel decision question` (`the day` 11 6 2026) EQUALS `the day` 13 7 2026

-- The s 192 rule: decided 11 June 2026, told on day 32 (Monday 13 July) is in time; day 33 is not.
`told on day 32` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the s 192 notice duty for a panel decision on` (`the day` 11 6 2026)) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the National Board` `give the complainant written notice of the panel's decision (s 192(2)(b))` 32))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `told on day 32` EQUALS TRUE
`told on day 33` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the s 192 notice duty for a panel decision on` (`the day` 11 6 2026)) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the National Board` `give the complainant written notice of the panel's decision (s 192(2)(b))` 33))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `told on day 33` EQUALS FALSE
-- An event that is not the duty's action does not fulfil it.
`a different action` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the s 192 notice duty for a panel decision on` (`the day` 10 6 2026)) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the National Board` `conduct the preliminary assessment of the referred matter (s 150(1))` 5))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `a different action` EQUALS FALSE

-- s 187: submissions to a panel.
#ASSERT `Can I make a submission to the panel question` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: as the complainant you may make a submission to the panel about the matter, with the panel's leave (s 187); the hearing is not open to the public (s 189)`
#ASSERT `Can I make a submission to the panel question` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not without the panel's leave: a complainant may make a submission only with the leave of the panel (s 187)`
#ASSERT `Can I make a submission to the panel question` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: section 187 lets the complainant make a submission only where the matter relates to the complaint`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ss 193-193C: must the Board tell the health ombudsman. Arguments: Board believes serious, a panel told the Board,
-- the health ombudsman already knows.
`must tell HO` MEANS `yes: the National Board must notify the health ombudsman as soon as practicable (s 193(1)); the health ombudsman must then ask the Board either to refer the matter to it, to be dealt with under the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 (ss 193(2)(a), 193A(1)), or to continue under this Law, when the Board may refer it to a responsible tribunal (ss 193(2)(b), 193B(2)) unless it decides there is no public interest in a hearing (s 193C); a referral about your complaint must include your details (s 193B(4))`
#ASSERT `Must the Board tell the health ombudsman question` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `must tell HO`
-- s 193(1A): the health ombudsman referred it, so already knows.
#ASSERT `Must the Board tell the health ombudsman question` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the Board need not notify the health ombudsman on its own belief, because it is satisfied the health ombudsman already knows of the matter (s 193(1A)), for example because the health ombudsman referred it`
-- s 193(1A) excuses only paragraph (1)(a): a panel's notice under s 190(2) must still be passed on.
#ASSERT `Must the Board tell the health ombudsman question` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `must tell HO`
#ASSERT `Must the Board tell the health ombudsman question` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `must tell HO`
#ASSERT `Must the Board tell the health ombudsman question` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 193(1) arises only after the Board forms a reasonable belief of professional misconduct or another ground for suspension, cancellation or withdrawal, or a panel tells the Board it believes so (s 190(2))`

-- s 193B(3) with ss 6 and 9 of the Act: which tribunal.
#ASSERT `Which tribunal hears a Board referral question` `only in Queensland` FALSE EQUALS `QCAT, the responsible tribunal for Queensland (s 193B(3)(a)(i); s 6 of the Act)`
-- Behaviour only in Queensland by a practitioner based in New South Wales still goes to QCAT.
#ASSERT `Which tribunal hears a Board referral question` `only in Queensland` TRUE EQUALS `QCAT, the responsible tribunal for Queensland (s 193B(3)(a)(i); s 6 of the Act)`
#ASSERT `Which tribunal hears a Board referral question` `only in one other participating jurisdiction (another State or Territory)` TRUE EQUALS `the responsible tribunal of the other State or Territory where the behaviour occurred (s 193B(3)(a)(i))`
#ASSERT `Which tribunal hears a Board referral question` `in more than one jurisdiction` TRUE EQUALS `QCAT, because the behaviour occurred in more than one jurisdiction and the practitioner's principal place of practice is in Queensland (s 193B(3)(a)(ii); s 6 of the Act)`
#ASSERT `Which tribunal hears a Board referral question` `in more than one jurisdiction` FALSE EQUALS `the responsible tribunal of the State or Territory of the practitioner's principal place of practice, because the behaviour occurred in more than one jurisdiction (s 193B(3)(a)(ii))`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 129: insurance. Arguments: registration, practising, compliant cover.
`practising registration` MEANS `practising registration (general, specialist, provisional or limited)`
#ASSERT `Did the practitioner have to be insured question` `practising registration` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: they must not practise unless appropriate professional indemnity insurance arrangements are in force (s 129(1)); they had them`
#ASSERT `Did the practitioner have to be insured question` `practising registration` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes, and they were not: practising without appropriate professional indemnity insurance arrangements breaches s 129(1); it is not an offence but may lead to health, conduct or performance action (s 129(4)), and you may notify the health ombudsman on that ground (s 144(1)(e))`
#ASSERT `Did the practitioner have to be insured question` `practising registration` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 129 does not apply while the practitioner is not practising the profession`
#ASSERT `Did the practitioner have to be insured question` `non-practising registration` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 129 does not apply to non-practising registration (s 129(5))`
#ASSERT `Did the practitioner have to be insured question` `student registration` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 129 applies to a registered health practitioner, which by definition excludes registration as a student (s 5)`
#ASSERT `Did the practitioner have to be insured question` `not registered` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 129 applies only to a registered health practitioner (s 5)`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 237: protection from liability.
#ASSERT `Am I protected from liability question` `made a notification (complaint) to the health ombudsman about a registered health practitioner or student` TRUE EQUALS `yes: you are not liable civilly, criminally or under an administrative process for it, it is not a breach of professional etiquette or ethics, and no liability for defamation is incurred (s 237(1)-(3))`
-- The theatre nurse gave an investigator information.
#ASSERT `Am I protected from liability question` `gave information in an investigation, or for another purpose under this Law, to a person exercising functions under it` TRUE EQUALS `yes: you are not liable civilly, criminally or under an administrative process for it, it is not a breach of professional etiquette or ethics, and no liability for defamation is incurred (s 237(1)-(3))`
-- The nurse told Mere, who notified.
#ASSERT `Am I protected from liability question` `gave the person who notified the information the notification was based on, or was otherwise concerned in making it` TRUE EQUALS `yes: the protection extends to a person who, in good faith, gave the notifier the information or was otherwise concerned in the notification (s 237(4))`
#ASSERT `Am I protected from liability question` `made a notification (complaint) to the health ombudsman about a registered health practitioner or student` FALSE EQUALS `no: section 237 protects only a person acting in good faith (s 237(1), (4))`
#ASSERT `Am I protected from liability question` `gave the person who notified the information the notification was based on, or was otherwise concerned in making it` FALSE EQUALS `no: section 237 protects only a person acting in good faith (s 237(1), (4))`

-- s 56AA of the Act: ss 237A and 237B are omitted in Queensland.
#ASSERT `Does the National Law (Queensland) give this protection question` `protection from a reprisal (for example being sacked or threatened) for complaining or giving information` EQUALS `no: section 237A (reprisals) is omitted from the National Law (Queensland) by s 56AA of the Act; in Queensland, reprisals are dealt with by the Health Ombudsman Act 2013, ss 261-263 (an offence, and a tort giving damages), not encoded here`
#ASSERT `Does the National Law (Queensland) give this protection question` `protection from a confidentiality (non-disclosure) clause that would stop me complaining` EQUALS `no: section 237B (non-disclosure agreements) is omitted from the National Law (Queensland) by s 56AA of the Act, and s 329 by s 59; in Queensland, non-disclosure agreements are dealt with by the Health Ombudsman Act 2013, s 263A, not encoded here`
